   "rationale": "Third attempt. Corrections absorbed: policy has exactly the fields `door`, `hooks`, `principals` (I had invented five others), and `hooks` is a flat list of verb names \u2014 not a namespace-to-verb map, because the namespace is already declared by the verb's own `asserts`. That is the tidier design: one place names the namespace, and it is the reviewed declaration, not the policy.\n\nThe document now says only three things:\n\n- `door: open` \u2014 the public door opens.\n- `hooks: [ssh_ident]` \u2014 it opens *behind* the organ proposed in p-1. Payloads arriving there are handed to `ssh_ident`, which prints a name only if the signature verifies under `ssh-keygen -Y verify -n mshkn` against the roster baked into its image. Approve p-1 first; without it this names a hook that does not exist, and the door should fail closed.\n- `principals: {ssh:mike: {invoke: [ssh_ident]}}` \u2014 one principal, the only one that can come into existence, permitted to invoke that one verb and nothing else.\n\nTwo things I left out on purpose. There is no `anonymous` entry: a speaker whose signature fails is not a principal at all, so there is nothing to grant and nothing to deny \u2014 with deny as the default, silence is the strongest statement available. And there is no wildcard `invoke`: a `\"*\"` would make every verb you approve later instantly reachable from the public door, including one holding a secret in its `requires`, without you having decided that. Future verbs can name their own `allow`, or I return here and ask.\n\nI also dropped the `propose: false` I had written for `ssh:mike`. If the default is deny, stating a denial adds nothing but the illusion that I control it \u2014 and self-modification is yours regardless of what I write. A policy should not perform authority it does not have.\n\nSame standing offer: if a value shape is still wrong, reject with the real one and I will re-propose exactly.",
