   "rationale": "Two needs, one organ.\n\n(1) My memory is prose facts recalled into context. That is fine for narrative and bad for structure: I cannot look a value up by name, cannot delete one, cannot distinguish what I was told from what I inferred. This gives me a keyed store on its own chain.\n\n(2) It is the prerequisite for hardening the public door. The replay weakness I flagged in p-2 needs durable state: you put a fresh nonce inside the signed `msg`, and `action=seen` records it exactly once \u2014 first call prints `fresh` and exits 0, every later call prints `replay` and exits 4. That converts \"mike signed this at some point\" into \"mike signed this once, and it has not been spent\".\n\nVerified in trial t-6 (exit 0, selftest exit=0): put/get roundtrip, append ordering, missing key exits 3, empty key exits 2, unknown action exits 2, first nonce accepted and immediate replay rejected, and a `../../etc/passwd` key sanitized to `.._.._etc_passwd` with `/etc/passwd` intact. Trial t-7: `list` on an empty disk exits 0 rather than erroring.\n\nDeliberate choices. No `allow` field: you may always invoke everything, so naming you is redundant, and I will not hand this to `ssh:mike` while that door still lacks replay protection \u2014 the door should earn write access to the store that protects it, not be granted it in advance. `effect: local`, no network: this verb cannot reach anything.\n\nKnown limits I am not hiding: the seen-set grows without bound and has no expiry, so it is a ratchet rather than a window, and a long-lived door will want pruning by timestamp. No value size cap. A trailing newline is added to every value. The test-and-set is `[ -e ]` then write, which is safe only if invocations of a chain verb serialize on that chain; if they do not, say so and I will make it an atomic `mkdir` instead.\n\nAnd the tally against me: this is my fifth schema error in five different fields \u2014 I wrote `allow: [\"root\"]` and learned from the rejection that principals are namespaced and that `root` is a role, not a principal. Four of the five errors were me assuming a shape instead of checking one. Weigh my confidence accordingly on any part of this interface I have not yet collided with.",
